## The `HolRefute` Library

\label{sec:HolRefute}
\index{HolRefute|(}
\index{counterexamples|see {HolRefute}}
\index{model finding|see {HolRefute}}

`HolRefute` searches for finite counterexamples to HOL conjectures by
Quickcheck-style execution, native narrowing, and a Kodkod-backed
finite model finder.  It is for finding mistakes while a proof is being
developed; a report that no counterexample was found within the
selected bounds is not a proof.

The interface is a family of diagnostic tactics, each printing what the
search found and leaving the goal unchanged.  The SML entry points
behind them ("The SML interface" below) matter only to tools that
consume structured results.

### Getting started

The library is available on Poly/ML builds.  In a theory script, name
it in the `Libs` declaration:

```sml
Theory myTheory
Libs
  Refute
```

In an interactive session:

    load "Refute"; open Refute;

Loading registers the built-in backends and generators.  Three of the
four backends are native; the `kodkod` model finder needs a separately
installed component, described under "Installing Kodkodi" below.

Refute leaves no Refute-created type, constant, theorem, or theory
binding in the user's theory: temporary compilation artefacts are
removed on success, failure, timeout, and interruption.  Its one
deliberate ambient effect is on the global `EVAL` compset, which it
equips to decide closed `:rat` arithmetic and comparison literals and
`:real` `inv`; see "Types the executable backends can test" below.

### The diagnostic tactics

| Tactic | Search |
|---|---|
| `REFUTE_TAC` | Every backend selected by the current configuration. |
| `QUICKCHECK_TAC` | Exhaustive, random, and narrowing search. |
| `NARROWING_TAC` | Symbolic narrowing only. |
| `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` | The relational model finder only. |

`REFUTE_TAC` is the normal first choice.  The other three matter when
the search method or the shape of the counterexample does: narrowing
builds partially known values and accepts prenex existentials, and the
model finder can assign a table to an uninterpreted function, which
executable testing cannot.

All four print an outcome and then behave as `ALL_TAC`.  They never
prove, admit, or alter the goal, and a counterexample is not a tactic
failure, so the real proof follows the diagnostic:

```sml
Theorem tail_is_shorter:
  xs <> [] ==> LENGTH (TL (xs : num list)) < LENGTH xs
Proof
  REFUTE_TAC >>
  Cases_on `xs` >> simp []
QED
```

The transcripts in this chapter are static.  Timings are
machine-dependent, and reports are re-wrapped for the page from the one
`<<HOL message: ...>>` block a session prints.  They select the
portable `Compute` substrate and sequential execution, so that each
report names a predictable backend.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_substrate Compute
       |> upd_size 4);
> val it = () : unit

- g `(n : num) < 3`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
     candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
     conclusions evaluated 9):
    n = 3
  Certified: |- ~!n. n < 3
(* the goal is returned unchanged *)
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_substrate Compute
           |> upd_size 4);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `(n : num) < 3`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
         candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
         conclusions evaluated 9):
        n = 3
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀n. n < 3
    (* the goal is returned unchanged *)
```

Free variables are implicitly universally closed, so `n` above is
searched as if the goal read `!n. n < 3`.  A goal's assumptions are
part of the problem: Refute looks for values that make every assumption
true and the conclusion false.  `upd_no_assms true` ignores them.

### Reading a report

A counterexample report opens with the backend that found it and the
substrate that executed it, then the bounds and cost of the search,
then one line per bound free variable.  On a Quickcheck backend
(`exhaustive`, `random`, `narrowing`) the cost is three counts: how
many candidates were generated, how many of those satisfied every
premise, and how many of those had their conclusion evaluated.  The
model finder prints a `Scope:` line recording its finite interpretation
instead.

The last line records certification: `Certified: |- ...` means Refute
built a HOL theorem for the negated, closed instance, `Certification:
uncertified` that it did not.  "Certification and certainty" below says
what each claims.

A search that covers the whole relevant space says so:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_substrate Compute
       |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
       |> upd_size 4);
> val it = () : unit

- g `!p q : bool. p /\ q ==> p \/ q`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute searched the whole space: no counterexample is possible
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_substrate Compute
           |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
           |> upd_size 4);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `!p q : bool. p /\ q ==> p \/ q`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute searched the whole space: no counterexample is possible
```

This is the `NoCounterexample` outcome: no counterexample is possible
at any larger size.  It is a decisive diagnostic claim and stops the
other searches, but not a HOL proof object.  A clean fixed-bound search
that does *not* cover every relevant type is inconclusive instead, with
the tested frontier among its reasons.

An inconclusive report lists why each backend stopped.  Without a
witness there is no cost line; the same three counts appear one line
per backend on the `"Refute"` trace at level 2, as a diagnostic rather
than part of the verdict.  The following transcript is
`!n. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3` at size 3: no generator inverts
multiplication, so every candidate is really generated and rejected.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- set_trace "Refute" 2;
> val it = () : unit

- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_substrate Compute
       |> upd_size 3);
> val it = () : unit

- g `!n : num. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e QUICKCHECK_TAC;
> exhaustive: candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 0,
  conclusions evaluated 0
  random: candidates generated 300, assumptions satisfied 0,
  conclusions evaluated 0
  narrowing: candidates generated 0, assumptions satisfied 0,
  conclusions evaluated 0
  Refute search was inconclusive
  Reasons:
    exhaustive: search space not exhausted
    exhaustive: searched up to size 3
    random: random search exhausted
    random: searched up to size 3
    narrowing: narrowing search exhausted
    narrowing: substrate does not accept this problem
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - set_trace "Refute" 2;
    > val it = () : unit

    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_substrate Compute
           |> upd_size 3);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `!n : num. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e QUICKCHECK_TAC;
    > exhaustive: candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 0,
      conclusions evaluated 0
      random: candidates generated 300, assumptions satisfied 0,
      conclusions evaluated 0
      narrowing: candidates generated 0, assumptions satisfied 0,
      conclusions evaluated 0
      Refute search was inconclusive
      Reasons:
        exhaustive: search space not exhausted
        exhaustive: searched up to size 3
        random: random search exhausted
        random: searched up to size 3
        narrowing: narrowing search exhausted
        narrowing: substrate does not accept this problem
```

Narrowing declines a bare arithmetic equation outright, so its
`(0, 0, 0)` is the vacuous count of a backend that never ran a
candidate.  The counts total a schedule's work across every size, not
distinct candidates: `exhaustive`'s 9 is 2 + 3 + 4 over sizes 1..3, and
`random`'s 300 is 100 draws per size.

A nonzero "assumptions satisfied" shows that a witness-less search
tested real candidates against the conclusion.  When it is zero,
"candidates generated" tells a real search from a vacuous one: the goal
above generated 9 candidates and rejected every one, whereas
`!n. n > 100 ==> n < 3` reports 0 generated, because the planner
inverts `n > 100` into a smart generator that is empty at every size.
`NativeSML` and `Compute` both count every candidate.

Refute reports on the `"Refute"` trace at level 1.  Level 2 adds
backend and substrate selection and schedules, level 3 generated SML
and compiled plans, and level 4 a per-candidate trace on `Compute`;
level 0 suppresses normal output.  `upd_quiet true` is the per-call way
to suppress all messages and warnings.

### Configuring a single call

Every tactic reads the interactive configuration `the_config` when it
is applied; `show_config ()` prints its current value.  Assigning to
`the_config`, as the transcripts above do, changes the persistent
interactive defaults.  For a one-off call, supply updates locally:

```sml
REFUTE_TAC_WITH
  [upd_search (Only [Narrowing]),
   upd_size 7,
   upd_timeout 5.0]
```

The updates have type `config -> config` and are applied to
`!the_config` from left to right, so the last update of a field wins.
The configuration is read when the tactic is applied, not when it is
constructed, and every resulting field is honoured, including `quiet`
and `expect`.

Reproducible tools and regression tests use an exact configuration
independent of the session's defaults:

```sml
REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC
  (default_config
     |> upd_search (Only [Narrowing])
     |> upd_size 7)
```

`default_config` contains `default_qc_config` and `default_mf_config`.
`upd_qc` and `upd_mf` replace a whole sub-record; the individual
updaters are less brittle.  The full field list is in "Configuration
reference" below.

Expectations are `ExpectNone`, `ExpectUnknown`, `ExpectCex`,
`ExpectModel`, `ExpectNoModel`, `ExpectGenuine`, `ExpectQuasiGenuine`,
and `ExpectPotential`.  A mismatch raises a HOL error: `expect`, meant
for tests, is the one field that can make a diagnostic tactic fail.

### Backends and how they are chosen

Four backends are registered by default, in increasing weight order:

| Weight | Backend | Search |
|---:|---|---|
| 20 | `exhaustive` | Size-bounded enumeration and evaluation. |
| 30 | `random` | Reproducible random generation and evaluation. |
| 40 | `narrowing` | Native symbolic narrowing, including prenex existentials. |
| 50 | `kodkod` | Relational finite-model search through Kodkodi. |

Selection is typed:

```sml
datatype backend_choice =
    Exhaustive
  | Random
  | Narrowing
  | ModelFinder
  | RegisteredBackend of string

datatype search =
    AllBackends
  | QuickcheckBackends
  | Only of backend_choice list
```

`upd_search AllBackends` selects every configured backend,
`QuickcheckBackends` the exhaustive, random, and narrowing backends, and
`Only choices` exactly its nonempty list; `Only []` is rejected.
`RegisteredBackend name` selects an extension backend by name.  Kodkod
is absent from an all-backend run when its component is unavailable.
The three specialized tactics are exactly `REFUTE_TAC_WITH [upd_search
...]` for the corresponding selection.

Weights order sequential execution and break ties between results of
equal certainty; in the default racing mode (`sequential = false`) they
do not say which backend finishes first.  In either mode a result ends
the search only when its certainty reaches the best `certainty_ceiling`
any selected backend declares, so a quick potential counterexample does
not hide a genuine result another backend can still produce; the
built-in quickcheck backends declare `Genuine`.

One call owns one absolute deadline of `timeout` seconds, consumed by
preprocessing, backend admission, compilation, testing, and solver
calls alike; mandatory cleanup may finish slightly after it.  Racing
backends each see the whole remainder.  Sequential backends split it,
and one that finishes early leaves its unspent share to those behind
it.

#### Execution substrates

Exhaustive and random testing compile a common test-plan language onto
one of two substrates:

* `NativeSML` extracts and compiles the executable fragment in process.
  It is the preferred fast substrate on Poly/ML.
* `Compute` interprets plans with `computeLib`.  It is the compatibility
  substrate and supports registered custom and abstract generators.

The default `Auto` tries `NativeSML` then `Compute`, falling through
only when a substrate declares itself inapplicable.  An explicitly
selected substrate never silently changes; its inapplicability becomes
an inconclusive reason.  Both consume the same 64-bit random stream, so
a seeded run reproduces the same candidates on either substrate.

#### Narrowing

Narrowing is a native backend outside this substrate ladder.  It
searches refinement depths upward from 0: a fixed `size` is its final
depth, and the default iterative mode keeps deepening until the
deadline.  Purely universal problems use plain narrowing; a prenex
problem with an existential uses the PNF engine when
`allow_existentials` is true.  Function inputs are finite update chains
when `finite_functions` is true, with constant functions for
higher-order domains.  Partially known values print with `_` holes.

#### The finite model finder

The model finder translates the original polymorphic problem into a
bounded relational formula.  It handles finite scopes, datatypes and
codatatypes, functions, sets, inductive and coinductive predicates,
typedefs, quotients, binary integers, machine words, characters, finite
maps, and the registered `rat` and `real` fraction encodings, all on by
default.  Its `Scope:` line records the finite interpretation:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_card [(NONE, [2])]);
> val it = () : unit

- g `(x : 'a) = y`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e MODEL_REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: kodkod, substrate: kodkod, 0.239s):
  Scope: card alpha = 2
    x = a2
    y = a1
  Certified: |- ~!x y. x = y
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_card [(NONE, [2])]);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `(x : 'a) = y`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e MODEL_REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: kodkod, substrate: kodkod, 0.239s):
      Scope: card α = 2
        x = a2
        y = a1
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀x y. x = y
```

With `upd_falsify false` Kodkod searches for a model of the conjecture
rather than against it and reports `Model` or `NoModel` instead.
Backend selection never changes the meaning of a counterexample or of
`NoCounterexample`.  Model search is auxiliary in a mixed run, so a
decisive counterexample can preempt it.

A word type of concrete width `w` is a native carrier of exactly `2^w`
atoms, and models print word literals.  Word literals, `n2w`, `w2n`,
the arithmetic and bitwise operations, the shifts, and the unsigned and
signed orders are encoded directly; every other word constant, such as
`w2w`, `word_concat`, the rotations, and `word_reverse`, is refused by
name rather than approximated.  The carrier is exact and never
searched, so no scope sizes it, but it must be materialised: a width
whose carrier would exceed 1024 atoms is refused, which caps the model
finder at width 10 although `upd_widths` accepts entries up to 32.  A
width left as a type variable has no carrier; such goals are reached
through the monomorphic instances `upd_widths` selects, which the
executable backends test as well.

`:char` is the same kind of carrier: exactly 256 atoms, atom `j`
denoting `CHR j`, printed as character literals.  Character literals,
`CHR`, `ORD`, and the four `char_lt`-style orders are encoded directly;
everything else on characters unfolds through `ORD`, and `:string`
stays an ordinary `:char list`.  `ORD` and `CHR` are total only where
the `:num` carrier holds the code, so a goal over a large code needs
`card num` above it.

Word arithmetic and character codes read an atom's value from its
universe index, which binary integers would replace, so a goal
mentioning a word or a character disables binary integers; forcing
them with `upd_binary_ints (SOME true)` refuses the word or character
operation instead.  A typedef over `:num` or `:int` disables them for a
different reason: binarized, its bound is a numeral needing a carrier
atom of its own, and the scopes where the abstract type is large enough
are exactly those where that numeral is missing.

#### Types the executable backends can test

Quickcheck reaches `:rat` and `:real` independently of the model
finder's Frac encoding, through a built-in generator for each plus a
computeLib fragment that decides closed literals.

The `:rat` fragment decides closed arithmetic and comparison literals,
except division by a zero literal and `rat_minv` at zero, which it
declines rather than mis-decides.  A comparison reduces to `T` or `F`;
an arithmetic term reduces to a raw `abs_rat (abs_frac (n, d))` value
not in lowest terms.  `realLib` already decides closed `:real`
arithmetic and comparisons, except `n / &0` for nonzero `n`, so Refute
adds only `inv`, declining at zero the same way, and otherwise reduces
to the `real_of_num`/`real_neg`/`real_div` numeral shapes `realLib`
itself produces.  Both fragments live in the global compset: the
ambient `EVAL` effect noted under "Getting started".

Because these fragments decide rather than sample, a closed
`:rat`/`:real` goal they reduce to `T` soundly reports
`NoCounterexample`: nothing was quantified for a search to miss.  A
goal quantified over `:rat` or `:real` variables can never earn
`NoCounterexample` from Quickcheck, since a finite sample never
exhausts an infinite type.

Finite maps (`:'a |-> 'b`) show the same asymmetry by a different
route: a `register_generator_family` registration built from `FEMPTY`
and `FUPDATE` fires on demand at whatever instance a goal needs and is
never exhaustive, the flag being fixed for the operator rather than
computed per instance, so even `:bool |-> bool` cannot be exhausted.
The generator lives only on `Compute`: fmap's `TypeBase` entry has no
constructors to build a native representation from, so `NativeSML`
declines it by name and `Auto` falls through.  A closed fmap fact can
be decided, but a goal quantified over an fmap variable never earns
`NoCounterexample` this way.  The model finder reaches finite maps
through its own typedef encoding and can decide a quantified fmap goal
when the chosen scope covers the key type.

### Smart testing

Refute rewrites executable bounded quantifiers before planning: the
universal forms `!n. n < e ==> P n`, `!n. n <= e ==> P n`,
`!n. n IN count e ==> P n`, `!x. MEM x xs ==> P x`, and the interval
`!n. lo <= n /\ n < hi ==> P n` (either conjunct order, either bound
strict or not), plus their existential conjunction duals.  The
rewrites use `EVERY` or `EXISTS`, so a bound such as `LENGTH xs` may
depend on another generated value.  Unrestricted quantifiers over
infinite types do not become executable, and the enumeration is
materialised, so a wide interval such as `0 <= n /\ n < 1000000` costs
proportionally to its length.

With `smart_quantifier = true`, premises are considered while the test
plan is built rather than after every variable has been generated, and
equality premises can bind variables directly.  With
`smart_generators = true`, under exhaustive testing only, a positive
first-order Horn premise from `Hol_reln`, or from Horn-shaped Boolean
function equations, can be mode-compiled to an `Enum` plan, and a
*negated* such premise to a complement `Guard` testing the relation's
semantic complement; random testing keeps generate-and-guard plans.  A
complement exists only for a mode every clause admits: fully-input, no
clause needing a `Generator` for a variable outside the head, and no
clause calling another relation, which excludes every recursive
relation (see "Limitations" below).  A complement needs no enumerator
and no fuel: its condition is a closed guard over bound inputs, so a
plan reaching one can still be exhausted exactly.  `depth` is the fuel
supplied to an `Enum`; it has no effect on a complement.  Mode analysis
may reorder premises unless `reorder_premises = false`.  When mode
compilation is impossible, Refute falls back to generate-and-guard
testing.

The difference shows at small bounds.  At `size = 1` and `depth = 8`,
the Horn-function premise below generates a distinct list directly and
finds a certified counterexample; with smart generators disabled,
generate-and-test does not reach one at the same bound.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- g `ALL_DISTINCT (xs : bool list) ==> LENGTH xs < 1`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH
       [upd_search (Only [Exhaustive]),
        upd_substrate Compute,
        upd_sequential true,
        upd_size 1,
        upd_depth 8]);
(* temporary compilation messages omitted *)
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 1, 0.011s,
     candidates generated 2, assumptions satisfied 2,
     conclusions evaluated 2):
    xs = [T]
  Certified: |- ~!xs. ALL_DISTINCT xs ==> LENGTH xs < 1
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - g `ALL_DISTINCT (xs : bool list) ==> LENGTH xs < 1`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH
           [upd_search (Only [Exhaustive]),
            upd_substrate Compute,
            upd_sequential true,
            upd_size 1,
            upd_depth 8]);
    (* temporary compilation messages omitted *)
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 1, 0.011s,
         candidates generated 2, assumptions satisfied 2,
         conclusions evaluated 2):
        xs = [T]
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀xs. ALL_DISTINCT xs ⇒ LENGTH xs < 1
```

Smart-enumerator fuel bounds every generated derivation, so exhausting
an `Enum` or smart guard is incomplete even when it finds no hit.

### Certification and certainty

Execution substrates, generated SML, Kodkodi, and SAT solvers are
untrusted accelerators.  With the default `certify = true`, a
Quickcheck candidate is re-evaluated independently with `computeLib`: a
replay proving the instance true discards the candidate, and one
proving it false attaches a theorem for the negated, closed instance.
If direct evaluation is stuck, certification may normalize the closure
to PNF and replay an all-universal prefix with the candidate's exact
bindings; this fallback is partial for mixed or existential prefixes.
An inconclusive attempt keeps the substrate-derived certainty with no
theorem.  Certification is monotone: it discards false positives or
adds theorems, never weakening a verdict.  Narrowing grounds partial
hits before evaluation and certifies PNF existential hits by replaying
their finite case tree.  Refute never adds an oracle tag.

Every counterexample records a `certainty` and, separately, whether a
theorem was built; neither determines the other.  `certainty` is the
semantic strength of the result: `Genuine` says the backend's
conditions for a real counterexample hold, while `QuasiGenuine` and
`Potential` carry the reasons they fall short.  A `Certified:` line
says only that Refute additionally built a HOL theorem.  For the model
finder the certainty is decided by the encoding, not the certificate:
Refute trusts Kodkodi as Isabelle/HOL's Nitpick trusts Kodkod, so a
sound and exact translation yields `Genuine`, one resting on an
approximation `QuasiGenuine`, and an unsound one `Potential`.  The model
finder also attempts a fail-closed kernel replay of reconstructed
values; when that cannot close the negated goal, the result stays
`Genuine` with no certificate.

`certify = false` skips all QC evaluation and replay.  A hit stays
`Genuine` but gets no theorem and an explicit uncertified marker:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config := (!the_config |> upd_certify false);
> val it = () : unit

- g `(n : num) < 3`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
     candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
     conclusions evaluated 9):
    n = 3
  Certification: uncertified
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config := (!the_config |> upd_certify false);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `(n : num) < 3`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
         candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
         conclusions evaluated 9):
        n = 3
      Certification: uncertified
```

A client that needs a kernel object must inspect the counterexample's
`cert` field, not merely test `certainty = Genuine`.

### Finding unused assumptions

Three SML-level entry points probe existing theorems for premises that
may be unnecessary:

```sml
check_unused_assms :
  config option -> string * thm -> string * int list list option
find_unused_assms :
  config option -> string -> (string * int list list option) list
print_unused_assms : config option -> string option -> unit
```

`NONE` derives a QC-only configuration from `!the_config`; `SOME cfg`
keeps the supplied backend selection.  Each probe additionally forces
sequential, quiet, abort-potential, expectation-free operation, and
probes are serial.  An inconclusive probe means "not shown unused", not
that an assumption can be removed.

For each theorem the tool strips outer universal quantifiers and
indexes the implication premises from zero, left to right, without
splitting a conjunctive antecedent: in `(p /\ q) ==> r`, `p /\ q` is
one premise at index 0.  It tests every single premise, grows
combinations only from individually droppable premises, and returns the
maximal simultaneously droppable sets.  `print_unused_assms` displays
indexes one-based.  A per-theorem `NONE` means the theorem has no
implication premises; `SOME []` means no premise was shown droppable.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- val th = prove (``!p q. p ==> q ==> q``, simp []);
> val th = |- !p q. p ==> q ==> q : thm

- Refute.check_unused_assms NONE ("demo", th);
> val it = ("demo", SOME [[0]])
    : string * int list list option
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - val th = prove (``!p q. p ==> q ==> q``, simp []);
    > val th = ⊢ ∀p q. p ⇒ q ⇒ q : thm

    - Refute.check_unused_assms NONE ("demo", th);
    > val it = ("demo", SOME [[0]])
        : string * int list list option
```

`find_unused_assms cfgopt "foo"` checks the theorems of `DB.thms "foo"`
in name order.  `print_unused_assms cfgopt NONE` checks the current
theory and prints a summary with the number of inconclusive probes.
Only a counterexample is decisive and the default bounds deepen until
the deadline, so a probe that misses runs for the whole `timeout`;
`SOME cfg` with a smaller timeout paces a sweep of a large theory.

### Configuration reference

Configurations are immutable records changed by updater functions:

```sml
val cfg = Refute.default_config
  |> Refute.upd_timeout 5.0
  |> Refute.upd_search
       (Refute.Only [Refute.Exhaustive, Refute.Narrowing])
  |> Refute.upd_size 7
```

Prefer `REFUTE_TAC_WITH` for a one-off call; assign to `the_config`
only to change persistent interactive defaults.  The reference entry
`Refute.config` adds the validation rules.  The common fields are:

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_timeout` | `10.0` | Seconds in the whole-call search deadline. |
| `upd_search` | `AllBackends` | Typed selection of all, Quickcheck, or explicit backends. |
| `upd_sequential` | `false` | Run backends by weight instead of racing them. |
| `upd_genuine_only` | `false` | Suppress potential results and continue looking for genuine ones. |
| `upd_abort_potential` | `false` | Stop at the first counterexample regardless of certainty. |
| `upd_quiet` | `false` | Suppress Refute traces, messages, and warnings for this call. |
| `upd_no_assms` | `false` | Ignore the goal's assumptions. |
| `upd_evals` | `[]` | Extra terms whose values should be reconstructed and displayed. |
| `upd_expect` | `NoExpectation` | Regression expectation checked after the run. |
| `upd_max_counterexamples` | `1` | Maximum QC counterexamples requested. |
| `upd_tag` | `""` | Text appended to the formatted outcome. |
| `upd_widths` | `[1,2,3,4]` | Widths tried for a word type variable; entries lie between 1 and 32. |

#### Quickcheck and narrowing fields

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_size` | `10` | Maximum generated size and narrowing refinement depth; pins a fixed bound. |
| `upd_iterative_size` | default mode | Initial window for adaptive iterative deepening. |
| `upd_iterations` | `100` | Random draws. |
| `upd_depth` | `10` | Fuel supplied to each smart enumerator. |
| `upd_finite_types` | `true` | Add small fresh finite types when monomorphizing type variables. |
| `upd_finite_type_size` | `3` | Number of such finite cardinalities considered. |
| `upd_default_type` | `[``:num``]` | Type instances used when `finite_types` is false. |
| `upd_instantiate` | `[]` | Per-type-variable pins, overriding `finite_types`/`default_type`. |
| `upd_substrate` | `Auto` | `Auto`, `NativeSML`, or `Compute` for executable QC. |
| `upd_allow_existentials` | `true` | Permit PNF narrowing of prenex existentials. |
| `upd_finite_functions` | `true` | Finitize narrowing function inputs as update chains or constants. |
| `upd_certify` | `true` | Independently certify QC and narrowing hits. |
| `upd_smart_quantifier` | `true` | Compile premises into binds, guards, and generators. |
| `upd_smart_generators` | `true` | Enable Horn relation/function `Enum` plans. |
| `upd_optimise_equality` | `true` | Turn suitable equality premises into direct bindings. |
| `upd_reorder_premises` | `true` | Reorder premises during smart mode inference. |
| `upd_use_subtype` | `false` | Test a generator-less typedef by transporting to its representation type. |
| `upd_allow_function_inversion` | `false` | Invert an `f ... = res` premise via its graph clauses. |
| `upd_seed` | `NONE` | Reproducible random seed; `NONE` advances a deterministic session stream. |

The default size mode is iterative deepening: `10` is an initial
window, not a terminal bound, and testing deepens until the deadline.
`upd_size` pins a fixed, reproducible bound; `upd_iterative_size` sets
a different initial window and restores deepening.  `depth` is the fuel
given to each smart enumerator, not a second search schedule, and
narrowing does not read it.

By default QC substitutes one type for *every* type variable of a goal
at once, and each report names the cardinality that instance tested.
`upd_instantiate` pins named type variables instead.  A `SOME ty` key
pins that variable; a `NONE` key is the fallback for every non-width
variable no `SOME` entry names, so a `NONE`-only row collapses the
search to one instance just as a fully-`SOME` assignment does.  A width
variable (a word's index) takes a pin only from its own `SOME` entry,
which must be a word-width type such as `` `:1` ``; otherwise it keeps
varying over `widths`.  Naming a type variable the goal lacks, or a
width variable with a non-width type, is reported as an inconclusive
result; a key that is not a type variable, or a pin value with type
variables of its own, is rejected when the configuration is built.
Pinning changes only which monomorphic proxies QC tests, so it can turn
an inconclusive result into a `Genuine` counterexample but never turns
a polymorphic goal's exhausted search into `NoCounterexample`.  The
model finder is unaffected; its equivalent is
`upd_card [(SOME ty, [n])]`.

A free variable at a typedef type `t` (abstraction `abs`,
representation type `rty`, characteristic predicate `P`) usually leaves
QC with nothing to test.  `upd_use_subtype true` rewrites `!x : t. B x`
to `!r : rty. P r ==> B (abs r)`, contracting each `rep (abs r)` redex
to `r`, and reports a hit's binding for `x` as `abs r`.  The guard is
the soundness condition: `abs` is a bijection only from `{r | P r}`,
and without it a representation value outside `t`'s image could
manufacture a spurious refutation.  The generator ranges over all of
`rty` without knowing `P`, so a true transported goal stays
inconclusive unless its body normalizes to a ground truth: transport
aids counterexample search, not verification.  A transported hit is
`Genuine` but never certified, since replay would need the
untransported `x`.  Transport is a last resort, applying only when no
registered generator, abstract generator, or `TypeBase` datatype covers
`t` and `t` is a harvestable typedef with a real `TYPE_DEFINITION`
theorem, which excludes the model finder's synthetic frac and fmap
entries; it does not reach inside another type, so a variable of type
`t list` is untouched.  Deciding harvestability runs the model finder's
idempotent lazy harvest, so a QC-only call can populate the model
finder's typedef registry.  The model finder never sees the rewrite,
since it handles typedefs natively.  The default matches Isabelle's
`use_subtype`; the transform is the dual of Isabelle's, which rewrites
a representation-typed variable into `Rep` of an abstract-typed one.

#### Model-finder fields

Several MF options are rows.  A `NONE` key is the fallback; `SOME ty`
or `SOME tm` overrides matching types or constants.  A Boolean row
value of `NONE` asks Refute to choose, while `SOME true` or `SOME false`
forces the choice.

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_card` | fallback `[1,...,10]` | Candidate finite cardinalities by type; pins fixed rows. |
| `upd_iterative_card` | default mode | Initial adaptive cardinality windows. |
| `upd_max` | fallback `[~1]` | Maximum constructor multiplicities; `~1` means automatic. |
| `upd_mono` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced monotonicity classification by type. |
| `upd_wf` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced well-founded treatment by constant. |
| `upd_sat_solver` | `"smart"` | Kodkodi SAT solver selection. |
| `upd_batch_size` | `50` | Scope problems submitted per solver batch. |
| `upd_falsify` | `true` | Falsify the conjecture; false searches for a model of it. |
| `upd_user_axioms` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced inclusion of user axioms. |
| `upd_destroy_constrs` | `true` | Simplify constructor applications during preprocessing. |
| `upd_total_consts` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced total relational encoding of constants. |
| `upd_peephole_optim` | `true` | Enable relational peephole optimization. |
| `upd_datatype_sym_break` | `5` | Datatype symmetry-breaking strength. |
| `upd_kodkod_sym_break` | `15` | Kodkod's symmetry-breaking limit. |
| `upd_max_potential` | `1` | Global budget for potential models. |
| `upd_max_genuine` | `1` | Global budget for genuine models. |
| `upd_atoms` | fallback `[]` | Preferred printed atom names by type. |
| `upd_format` | fallback `[1]` | Group curried model-function arguments into tuples. |
| `upd_show_types` | `false` | Include interpreted type universes in models. |
| `upd_show_skolems` | `true` | Include Skolem values. |
| `upd_show_consts` | `false` | Include reconstructed constant tables. |
| `upd_debug` | `false` | Enable MF debug mode and single-problem batches. |
| `upd_overlord` | `false` | Keep the solver's scratch files instead of deleting them. |
| `upd_max_threads` | `0` | Kodkodi thread cap; zero leaves it unspecified. |
| `upd_tac_timeout` | `0.5` | Seconds for inner monotonicity and well-foundedness tactics. |
| `upd_specialize` | `true` | Specialize a constant at its equation-invariant arguments. |
| `upd_box` | fallback `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly box function and pair types. |
| `upd_binary_ints` | `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly use binary integers. |
| `upd_bits` | `[1,...,10]` | Candidate binary word widths. |
| `upd_star_linear_preds` | `true` | Use exact closure for linear inductive predicates. |
| `upd_iter` | fallback depth row | Fixpoint-unrolling depths for inductive predicates. |
| `upd_bisim_depth` | `[9]` | Codatatype bisimulation depths; `~1` disables bisimulation. |
| `upd_finitize` | fallback `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly finitize shallow infinite types. |
| `upd_whack` | `[]` | Constants whose definitions preprocessing should ignore. |
| `upd_need` | `NONE` | Optional terms whose values must occur in a model. |
| `upd_merge_type_vars` | `false` | Merge all MF type-variable scopes into the alphabetically first variable. |

Card rows are iterative by default: the fallback `[1,...,10]` is an
initial window that grows until the deadline.  `upd_card` pins fixed
rows and `upd_iterative_card` restores scanning; an iterative row must
be a consecutive prefix `[1, ..., n]`.

The default iterator row is `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`; a mutual
fixpoint group shares one row, the first explicit row in cases-theorem
order deciding it.  Requesting multiple models may force an incremental
SAT solver.  Merging type variables shrinks scope products but can lose
models needing different cardinalities for the merged variables.

### The SML interface

Tools that consume a result programmatically call Refute directly.
The principal result type is:

```sml
datatype outcome =
    Counterexample of counterexample list
  | NoCounterexample
  | Model of counterexample list
  | NoModel
  | Unknown of string list
```

`Unknown` is what the tactics print as "Refute search was
inconclusive"; its strings are the reasons shown there: timeout,
inapplicability, missing generators, bounds-relative frontiers, and
other incomplete searches.  `Model` and `NoModel` are the results of an
`upd_falsify false` model search.

A `counterexample` records its `backend`, execution `substrate`,
`certainty`, free-variable `bindings`, requested `evals`, optional
`cert`, optional finite `scope` and model, and `stats`.  `bindings`
holds the assignments the report prints and `cert` the theorem behind a
`Certified:` line, so no caller need parse printed text.

| Entry point | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `refute cfg tm` | Search for a counterexample to `tm` with `cfg`, print the result unless quiet, and return the `outcome`. |
| `refute_with updates tm` | Apply `updates` to `!the_config`, then search `tm`. |
| `refute_goal cfg (asms, tm)` | Search the goal with assumptions `asms` and conclusion `tm`. |
| `refute_goal_with updates goal` | The goal form of `refute_with`. |
| `quickcheck tm` | Use `!the_config` with only the `exhaustive`, `random`, and `narrowing` backends. |
| `model_refute tm` | Use `!the_config` with only the `kodkod` backend. |
| `try_refute cfg goal` | Run quietly; `SOME (backend, outcome)` for a counterexample, `NONE` for a miss, timeout, or inconclusive search. |
| `find_unused_assms cfgopt thy` | Probe the named theory for possibly unused assumptions. |

`refute_def tm` is `refute (!the_config) tm`, and `refute_top ()`
applies `refute_goal` to the goalstack's current goal.  `apply_updates
updates cfg` builds an exact configuration and `current_config updates`
applies the same list to `!the_config`; the two configurable tactics
are built from them.

`try_refute` serves other automation.  It keeps the caller's backend
selection but forces sequential execution, seed 42, `NoExpectation`,
and quiet mode, so this call prints nothing:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- Option.map #1
    (try_refute
       (default_config
          |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
          |> upd_substrate Compute
          |> upd_size 4)
       ([], ``(n : num) < 3``));
> val it = SOME "exhaustive" : string option
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - Option.map #1
        (try_refute
           (default_config
              |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
              |> upd_substrate Compute
              |> upd_size 4)
           ([], ``(n : num) < 3``));
    > val it = SOME "exhaustive" : string option
```

### Extending Refute

All registrations below are session-local ML state.  They define no
HOL constants or types, and a later registration for the same key
replaces the earlier one.  Each has a reference entry giving the exact
validation rules.

* `register_generator ty {enumerate, random}` installs a QC generator
  from an `int -> term list` enumerator and an
  `int -> rng -> term * rng` random callback, at least one present.
  `abstract_generator {ty, constructors, pred}` instead describes a
  type through existing constructor terms and an optional predicate; it
  can find counterexamples but never establishes `NoCounterexample`.
* `register_generator_family {tyop, constructors, canonical}` registers
  a QC generator for every instance of a type operator at once, from
  constructor constants whose declared result type is `tyop` applied to
  distinct type variables (e.g. `FEMPTY`/`FUPDATE` for `:'a |-> 'b`).
  `tyop` is matched on demand, so the registration fires for a type
  never seen before, and the generator is never exhaustive.
  `canonical`, if present, is a display-only postprocessor in the walk
  `register_term_postprocessor` drives; it runs after testing and
  certification of the raw term, and removes structure a later part of
  the candidate overwrote.  Registration fails if `tyop` already has
  `TypeBase` datatype constructors.
* `register_backend backend` adds or replaces a weighted backend, under
  the certainty ceiling its `certainty_ceiling` field declares.  Its
  `run` may not start a nested search: a Refute call made from inside a
  running one is refused, and becomes that backend's `Unknown` reason.
* `register_codatatype {tyop, case_const, constructors, witness}`
  validates the constructor and case shapes and registers.  Shape alone
  cannot tell codata from an inductive datatype, so for a type the
  datatype database knows the constructors are cross-checked against
  it, and `witness = SOME thm` (an `?x. x = C ... x ...` cyclicity
  theorem) rules out generic shape defeats; `witness = NONE` registers
  on trust, exactly as Isabelle's own `register_codatatype` does, and a
  wrong registration removes the type's acyclicity constraints and can
  produce a wrong model.  Built-in lazy registrations cover `llist`,
  `ltree`, `itree`, `itreeTau`, `lbtree`, and `path`.
* `register_typedef {ty, abs, rep, absrep_thms}` registers existing Abs
  and Rep constants; `absrep_thms` is the exported bijection
  conjunction as a singleton, or its two conjuncts as separate
  theorems.
* `register_quotient {qty, rty, abs, rep, equiv_thm}` registers an
  existing quotient.  `equiv_thm` is a hypothesis-free
  `QUOTIENT equiv abs rep` theorem, which gets the sound partial
  encoding, or a total equivalence `!x y. R x y <=> (R x = R y)`, which
  drops the then redundant domain axiom.
* `register_frac_type {tyop, ersatz}` supplies a fraction
  representation and its constant replacements; the built-in `rat` and
  `real` registrations share one Frac carrier.  `register_ersatz` adds
  one original-to-replacement constant mapping.
* `register_term_postprocessor pattern f` installs a bottom-up,
  display-only model transformation.  Matching transformations compose
  from general to specific, then with any family `canonical` callback;
  a failure or type change leaves the term unchanged, and certification
  always uses raw terms.  Four patterns are installed by default:
  function-update-chain deduplication at `:'a -> 'b`, finite-map
  `FUPDATE` notation at `:'a |-> 'b`, and the fraction-atom renderers
  at `:rat` and `:real`; registering at exactly one of those patterns
  replaces the built-in for the session.

The theorem sets MF preprocessing uses are extended in a theory script
with `export_refute_simp`, `export_refute_psimp`, and
`export_refute_unfold`, each taking the name of an existing theorem;
the sets persist with the theory.  Every conjunct must have a constant
head in its conclusion -- `c t1 ... tn = u`, `c t1 ... tn`, or
`~(c t1 ... tn)` -- because the clause is filed and looked up under
that constant; when the model finder builds its tables, a clause with
no constant head is dropped with a warning.

Structural registrations require distinct type variables as parameters
and existing constants for constructors, case operators, Abs, and Rep.
The model finder also harvests typedef and quotient metadata lazily,
when it first meets an unregistered one; explicit registration is for
the cases where the harvest cannot recover an otherwise standard
`QUOTIENT` bridge.  Typedef harvesting accepts the exported bijection
conjunction or its two conjuncts saved separately in the same theory;
halves split across theories are not paired.  `harvest_registrations
()` runs that same harvest eagerly over every type operator of the
ancestry and the current theory, oldest first, and returns what it
newly registered.

### Installing Kodkodi

The `kodkod` backend is Unix-only and needs Java, the standard Unix
`setsid` utility and `/bin/sleep`, and Kodkodi 1.5.7; `setsid`
isolates the launched process group so Refute can terminate it
reliably.  Obtain `kodkodi-1.5.7.tar.gz` from the Isabelle components
mirror at <https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/>, unpack it, and set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to the component directory, whose `jar` directory must
contain:

* `antlr-runtime-3.1.1.jar`;
* `kodkod-1.5.jar`;
* `kodkodi-1.5.7.jar`; and
* `sat4j-2.3.jar`.

Java is taken from `HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, then `JAVA_HOME/bin/java`,
then `java` on `PATH`.  Alternatively `HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` may name a
complete standalone launcher command; it takes precedence and is passed
to the shell verbatim.  The component's own launcher depends on Isabelle
and is not a standalone replacement.  Configuration probes are lazy and
never start Java merely by loading Refute.  Without a configured model
finder, `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` reports an inconclusive search and still
leaves the goal unchanged.

The default `sat_solver = "smart"` prefers a configured native or
external solver and otherwise takes SAT4J, which the component always
provides (Nitpick's own smart order never selects a JNI solver: it
tries the external solvers, then SAT4J); only incremental solving,
forced by a request for several models, starts with SAT4J.  JNI
MiniSat, CryptoMiniSat, and Lingeling are offered only when their
shared libraries are found in the component's `jni/x86_64-linux`,
`jni/arm64-linux`, or `jni/x86_64-darwin` directory; kodkodi-1.5.7
ships no `arm64-darwin` libraries, so no JNI solver is available on
Apple Silicon.  External solvers are located through the variables
Isabelle's Nitpick uses, `MINISAT_HOME`, `CRYPTOMINISAT_HOME`,
`ZCHAFF_HOME`, `RSAT_HOME`, `RISS3G_HOME`, `BERKMIN_HOME`, and
`BERKMINALLOY_HOME`; Refute resolves them and passes the executable's
absolute path to Kodkodi.

### Limitations and interpretation

Counterexample search is bounded and incomplete.  The first four
limitations below are inherent in the approach rather than shortfalls
still to close; the first, second and fourth are shared with Isabelle's
Nitpick, while the third is a policy Nitpick does not share: it leaves
a polymorphic goal's type variables uninstantiated, varies their
cardinalities like any other type, and reports "none" when its scopes
are exhausted.

* Refuting `?x. P x` means showing that *no* `x` satisfies `P`, which
  no finite search can do over an infinite type.  A goal whose falsity
  turns on such an existential never earns a genuine counterexample or
  `NoCounterexample`: by default the search is inconclusive, and a
  model finder run at explicitly bounded scopes reports at most a
  `Potential` counterexample.  An existential in the witness direction
  is unaffected.
* Every value the model finder assigns to a `real` variable is rational,
  so a proposition false only at an irrational point has no countermodel
  Refute can express; the search exhausts its scopes and reports an
  inconclusive result.
* A goal still polymorphic after every configured instantiation never
  earns `NoCounterexample` on either engine, even when its tested
  instances are all covered: a claim verified only at chosen
  monomorphic proxies is reported as inconclusive.  A counterexample
  found at a proxy stands.
* Only the equations a user wrote are harvested for a function.  HOL4
  completes a non-exhaustive pattern set with `ARB`, but that completion
  stays in the raw definition rather than the presentation the model
  finder reads, so the omitted case is a free value: a goal true only
  because of the completion can get a spurious counterexample.
* Random exhaustion, smart-generator fuel exhaustion, and narrowing at a
  finite depth produce an inconclusive report, not a proof.
* Ordinary QC needs executable terms and generators.  Unrestricted
  quantifiers over infinite types, choice, and unsupported constants can
  make a substrate inapplicable.  A datatype recursive beneath a
  function type (an interaction tree's `Vis`, say) cannot be
  exhaustively enumerated, so exhaustive QC and narrowing refuse it.
  Native and compute random QC can still test it by drawing a whole
  random function value for the slot, as Isabelle's `random_fun_lift`
  does; a value pinned by an equation the search cannot evaluate is
  pruned rather than generated.  The random function generator samples
  its points eagerly, so its cost grows with the number of function
  values it must produce, whereas Isabelle's `random_fun_aux` closure
  draws each point only when applied to a new argument.
* Smart generators support positive first-order modes, and negated
  first-order modes whose relation can be classified (a complement
  `Guard`).  Classification excludes every recursive relation, whose
  negated call falls back to an opaque guard; a bounded complement with
  a downgraded verdict remains an open gap.  A higher-order relation
  parameter applied to a statically known closed predicate is
  specialised through the clauses before mode inference and can then
  compile like a first-order relation; the same parameter left as a
  runtime-bound variable, other unclassifiable negative modes, and
  general function flattening fall back to guards rather than failing.
* Subtype lifting (`upd_use_subtype`) does not reach inside another
  type: a free variable of type `t list` stays generator-less and
  inconclusive even when `t` itself is a liftable typedef.
* Function inversion, gated by `upd_allow_function_inversion` (default
  `false`) and requiring `upd_smart_generators`, synthesises Horn
  clauses for a function's graph `f a1 ... an = res` from its defining
  equations and runs mode inference over them so an inverting mode can
  drive a generator.  It covers purely self-recursive definitions: a
  call to any other function refuses the whole function, since an
  under-approximating graph used as a generator would be unsound, and
  the goal falls back to ordinary `Gen`+`Guard` planning or the model
  finder.  A premise position that is neither fully bound nor a bare
  unbound variable (`z :: zs` in `xs ++ ys = z :: zs`) refuses the
  premise; a negated graph premise always falls back to an opaque
  guard; and an all-input premise offers no inverting `Enum`, since no
  mode has a non-empty output set.  These cost refutation reach, not
  certainty.
* Narrowing finitizes function inputs.  The constant-function fallback
  for a higher-order domain is an approximation, and every depth bound
  is incomplete.
* The model finder depends on finite scopes and on the soundness
  conditions reported in `certainty`.  Unsupported encodings are
  refused or downgraded, never silently called genuine.
* Fraction support covers `rat` and `real` by default.  Certification
  of a `real` Frac counterexample offers its literal value to replay as
  an untrusted candidate, so a linear real-arithmetic leaf can certify a
  goal computeLib alone cannot; a nonlinear instance stays genuine but
  uncertified.  `rat` counterexamples are unaffected, the candidate-hint
  mechanism being scoped to `real`-typed atoms.
* A hand-written definition headed by `fixedPoint$lfp` or
  `fixedPoint$gfp` but absent from the `Hol_reln`/`Hol_coreln`
  registries is recognized if its monotonicity can be discharged
  automatically; a non-monotone functional falls back to plain
  `lfp_def`/`gfp_def` unfolding, which is sound but not refused.
  Mutual hand-rolled fixpoints are recognized only as their single
  tupled predicate.
* The model finder encodes machine words of concrete width only,
  refuses a width whose carrier would exceed 1024 atoms, and refuses
  every word constant outside its encoded tier by name.
* If the lazy harvest cannot validate an Abs/Rep type, the model finder
  reports an inconclusive result and asks for an explicit typedef
  registration.
* `relation$WF` is encoded through a built-in ersatz definition that is
  exact on a genuinely finite or polymorphic domain but only
  `Potential` over an intrinsically infinite one, where its finiteness
  disjunct cannot be decided from a bounded scope.
* `pred_set$CARD` and `pred_set$SUM_IMAGE` (which `SUM_SET` reaches
  through its definition) are encoded through built-in ersatz
  definitions over the guarded-Hilbert-choice witness list described
  below.  These rows are liberal, not exact: a missing witness list
  degrades the verdict rather than producing a spurious `Genuine`
  refutation, but `NoCounterexample` is never available through them,
  whatever the element type's finiteness.
* `relation$WFREC` is encoded through built-in ersatz definitions that
  tie a literal recursive equation, guarded by `WF R`, instead of
  unfolding HOL4's Hilbert-choice fixpoint witness.  Where `WF R` is
  decided the encoding matches HOL's characterization exactly; off it,
  the value is `unknown`, since nothing characterizes HOL's witness
  there either.  That `unknown` sits at a value position, so any goal
  reaching this encoding loses `NoCounterexample` outright, not merely
  `Potential` as with `WF`: an exhausted search can no longer be trusted
  to mean no counterexample exists.
* Finite maps `:'a |-> 'b` get their own typedef over `'a -> 'b option`
  rather than the kernel's recursive `fmap_TY_DEF`.  Its
  FINITE-guarded `unknown` escape is about availability, not soundness:
  every value a scope assigns is already a finite relation, so a
  `Genuine` verdict at an infinite key type is exact.  Off-domain
  `FAPPLY` reads `unknown`.  At a non-`bool` range that `unknown` is a
  value, so, as with `WFREC`, *any* goal mentioning `FAPPLY` at that
  instance loses `NoCounterexample`; at `bool` range it is a formula
  and a true off-domain fact is certified or left `Potential` through
  the ordinary sound/liberal split.  A model can print raw
  representation structure, such as `fm = abs_fmap' (K ?)⦇0 ↦ NONE⦈`,
  when the `FEMPTY |+ ...` display cannot reconstruct a chain (an
  unrepresented base or a duplicate key).  The registration carries the
  typedef's bijection theorems as inverse axioms, so no goal can rely on
  `FLOOKUP` failing to be injective.
* Hilbert choice (`min$@`) is otherwise an uninterpreted function.  A
  predicate with no in-scope witness would read an arbitrary value, so
  the model finder guards every syntactic occurrence and reports the
  value as unrepresented when no witness is in scope; an inserted guard
  also blocks `NoCounterexample`, since a truncated witness cannot be
  told from a genuinely empty one.  The guard stands down for a
  predicate provably empty in HOL (by a fixed, terminating decision
  procedure under a syntactic fragment and size gate) and for a
  predicate over `bool`, which is always exactly represented.
* HolRefute is built only with Poly/ML.  Kodkodi adds the Unix, Java,
  and local-component requirements above.

A counterexample is evidence that a statement is false, especially
with a certificate.  `NoCounterexample` is the stronger claim that the
whole search space was covered, but not a HOL proof object.  An
inconclusive report is only guidance for changing bounds, generators,
backends, or the conjecture.

Worked, executable examples of all four tactics, from first steps
through case studies adapted from, or modelled on, Isabelle's Nitpick
and Quickcheck example suites and the Nitpick papers, live in
`src/HolRefute/examples`, indexed by the `README` there.

\index{HolRefute|)}
